--- !u!114 &989397222
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 989397220}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "How does a kiss feel like?\r\nA little sticky, warm, urgent. You don\u2019t
    like the rough scratch of stubble against your lips sometimes.\r\n\nBut Lana
    feels different. Her skin is soft, her breath warm and restless. You gently brush
    away the strands of hair on her cheek and kiss her lips\u2014minty, from her
    lipstick.\r\n\nYou never thought you\u2019d be kissing your best friend, in her
    bathroom. You were just trimming her bangs, and then, like you were under some
    kind of spell, you leaned in and kissed her.\r\n\nYou remember what they taught
    in class about consent\u2014damn it, you didn\u2019t ask her. But it happened
    so suddenly. It felt like\u2026a calling, and you just acted on it. But at least
    Lana didn\u2019t push you away, right?\r\n\n\"I\u2019m sorry! I\u2026did that\u2026
    you\u2026.\u201D\r\nLana doesn\u2019t say anything, but her face is burning red.\r\n\n\u201C\u2026Can
    I kiss you again, Lana?\u201D\r\nYou wait, excited and regretful. Lana hesitates,
    then slowly nods.\r\n\nYou smile. Mimicking Greg\u2019s habit, you turn on the
    faucet, letting the sound of running water mask the wild pounding of your heart.\r\n\n\u201CGirls!
    Come down and help set the table! Lana!\u201D\r\n\nAt the sound of her mom\u2019s
    voice, Lana jumps back instantly.\r\n\nYou watch her, mesmerized\u2014her flushed
    cheeks, her lips, as delicate as a rose.\r\n\n\u201CWe\u2019re coming, Mom!\u201D\r\n\nShe
    hurriedly smooths down the bangs you just trimmed.\r\n\n\u201CCome on, let\u2019s
    go.\u201D\r\n\nHer beautiful green eyes refuse to meet yours.\r\n\nYou love being
    around Lana, feeling her warmth. Just being near her makes you happy. But how
    is this any different from before? You\u2019re still friends\u2026 just that
    now, you crave her more.\r\n\nYou don\u2019t resist the urge. You take her hand\u2014right
    at the school gate. She stiffens for a second before quickly tucking her hand
    into her pocket.\r\n\n\u201CCan we go to your place?\u201D\r\n\nShe smiles.\r\n\nYou
    don\u2019t like that smile.\r\n\n\u201C...Sure.\u201D\r\n\n\u201CLana\u2026\u201D
    You say her name, nerves tightening in your chest like before an exam.\r\n\nShe\u2019s
    nervous too\u2014you can feel it in the slight dampness of her palm.\r\n\n\u201CWhat
    is it, Mad\u2026\u201D\r\n\n\u201CWill you\u2026 be my girlfriend?\u201D\r\n\n\u2026\r\n\nSilence.
    Suffocating silence.\r\n\n\u201CPlease, Lana, say something\u2026\u201D\r\n\n\u201CWe
    shouldn\u2019t. We are both\u2014\u201D\r\n\nYou cut her off.\r\n\n\u201CDo you
    not feel anything for me at all?\u201D\r\n\nAnger. Frustration. The sting of
    rejection swallows you whole.\r\n\nYou leave Lana behind and run.\r\n\nLana\u2019s
    quietness always infuriated you. You wanted her to chase after you, to yell at
    you, to feel something\u2014anything. But she just kept attending class, saying
    her prayers, even getting closer to other guys.\r\n\nWhatever. It\u2019s not
    like you didn\u2019t have admirers too.\r\n\nSo you played along, going out with
    the boys who pursued you.\r\n\n\nOf course, Lana never came looking for you.\r\n\nThe
    night before prom, you went to find her, a bouquet of roses in hand.\r\n\nBut
    what you saw was her, eyes closed, kissing another guy.\r\n\nYour Lana. Sweet,
    shy Lana. Your Lana.\r\n\nHow does a kiss feel like?\r\nBitter. Like the taste
    of your own tears slipping past your lips.\r\n\nThat semester, Lana moved away.\r\n\nWhen
    you saw her again, she had changed.\r\nVibrant red hair. Smudged smoky eyeliner.
    Those green eyes\u2014still pulling you back to that scorching summer when you
    were fourteen.\n\r\nBut the dark circles under them, barely concealed by foundation,
    told another story. She was more beautiful. More exhausted.\r\n\nYou didn\u2019t
    know why Lana chose to return to Crown Heights, or why she came to see you.\r\n\nYou
    had changed too. The curve of your belly. The absence of a ring on your finger.\r\n\nLana
    was always busy\u2014traveling for work, never staying long.\r\n\nYou two just
    had coffee. You didn\u2019t even order coffee, opting for hot tea instead\u2014because
    you wanted only the best for the child growing inside you.\r\n\n\u201CLana.\u201D\r\n\nBefore
    she left, you stopped her.\r\n\n\u201CIf you\u2019re free this weekend, would
    you come over?\u201D You fidgeted nervously. \u201CThere\u2019s\u2026 more I
    want to say.\u201D\r\n\nLana hesitated, then nodded. And left.\r\n\nThat weekend,
    as you waited\u2014anxious, expectant\u2014in your tiny apartment, you hit your
    stomach against the counter.\r\n\nYour water broke early.\r\n\nBy the time the
    ambulance arrived, you were biting down on your pain, forcing yourself to leave
    a note. God, you regretted not asking for Lana\u2019s number.\r\n\nYou told them
    to write down the hospital name and your condition\u2014before the agony swallowed
    you whole, and you blacked out.\r\n\nLana found the note.\r\n\nShe had never
    pressed the gas pedal so hard in her life.\r\n\nHer Mad. The brightest star of
    her youth.\r\n\nAnd in the final moment of her life, as the car flipped over,
    time seemed to freeze.\r\n\nThe bouquet of roses in her backseat tumbled, torn
    apart by the violent crash, petals scattering into the air and drop to the ground.
    \r\n\n\u2026\r\n\nYour hospital door opened.\r\n\nYour family surrounded you,
    stroking your sweat-soaked hair.\r\n\nThe baby cried softly beside you.\r\n\nShe
    never showed up.\r\n\nYou sighed.\r\n\nMaybe it was time to finally let go.\r"
