--- !u!114 &1478478500
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1478478498}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "\u201CShould I capture this rook or force the king to move first?\u201D\r\n\nYour
    childhood was spent staring at a chessboard\u2014black and white squares, like
    chocolate, cold and smooth to the touch. You always wanted to own a chessboard,
    not to play, but just to keep in your room and stare at it.\r\n\nUntil, one by
    one, the polished, delicate pieces were placed upon the board, and they became
    your puzzles to solve, your strategic dilemmas, your silent battlefield.\r\n\nBut
    that never troubled you. On the contrary, you loved the challenge.\r\n\nYou loved
    sitting in your chair, crossing your legs (until your mom smacked your knee),
    simulating battles in your head, and then\u2014picking up your piece, placing
    it down with certainty.\r\n\n\"Your turn.\"\r\nYou enjoyed taunting your opponent,
    showing off with a smug challenge.\r\n\nOn sleepless nights, you liked holding
    a chess piece in your hand, staring at the ceiling, picturing the board, replaying
    the day\u2019s matches\u2014until sleep finally claimed you. Who knows? Maybe
    your dreams would finish the game for you.\r\n\nYou thought you were born to
    be a professional chess player. A genius. Destined to carve your way forward
    and stand on bigger stages.\r\n\nUntil you were twelve, and a newly arrived player
    in your age group defeated you.\r\n\nFor the next two years, you lost. Again
    and again.\r\n\n\"Why?! I will defeat him!\" You swore to yourself. After all,
    you were supposed to be a chess prodigy\u2014how could you just be beaten like
    this?\r\n\nThat kid advanced to the bigger stage before you did. And then, in
    his first promotion match, he lost. Completely. Without a chance to fight back.\r\n\nYou
    fell silent. For the first time, you questioned your talent\u2014and your love
    for chess.\r\nAnd then, as it turned out\u2026 well, you don\u2019t really want
    to admit it.\r\nYou struggled to define chess: was it just a hobby, or was it\u2014like
    you had always believed\u2014a competition?\r\n\nAs you grew older, this question
    gnawed at you. What even is talent?!\r\nEvery time you thought your hard work
    had finally paid off, a new opponent would show up and crush your confidence
    again.\r\n\nHow much energy were you supposed to put into chess? Were you really
    meant to pursue the path of a professional player? How were you supposed to balance
    chess and the rest of your life?\r\nThere was no inspiring story, no miracle.\r\n\nJust
    like an old, dull poetry book, with half of its pages torn out\u2014no one cares
    enough to search for the missing half. And so, abandoned, the book gathers dust
    in the corner.\r\n\nYou gradually distanced yourself from chess tournaments.
    The chess set you got as a birthday gift\u2014the smooth board, carefully kept
    in the bookshelf\u2014was no longer touched.\r\n\n\"Mad, want to play a game?\"\r\nLana
    asks you.\r\n\nYou shake your head. There\u2019s an unexplainable discomfort.\r\n\n\"I
    don\u2019t play anymore.\"\n\r\nI don\u2019t want to play.\r\nI don\u2019t want
    to lose.\r\nI don\u2019t want to admit it.\r"
